---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import PhotoSlot from "../../components/PhotoSlot.astro";

const timeline = [
  { year: "2009", label: "First transplant, from a friend" },
  { year: "2014", label: "FSGS recurs; dialysis begins" },
  { year: "2019", label: "Matched, then cancer cancels surgery" },
  { year: "2021", label: "Transplanted via the donor chain" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Founder's Story"
  description="Robert Sanchez has needed two kidney transplants. This is the story behind The Jentosy Project's name and mission."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Founder's Story"
    heading="Two transplants. Two women who wouldn't give up. One organization named for both of them."
  />

  <section class="on-light section-tight">
    <div class="container narrow portrait-block">
      <PhotoSlot src="/images/founder-portrait.jpg" alt="Robert Sanchez, founder of The Jentosy Project" ratio="1 / 1" />
      <p class="linkline">Robert Sanchez, founder of The Jentosy Project.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="on-dark section-tight divider-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="timeline">
        {timeline.map((item) => (
          <div class="timeline-item">
            <div class="timeline-year">{item.year}</div>
            <div class="timeline-label">{item.label}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="on-light section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div>
        <p>
          In 2009, Robert Sanchez received his first kidney transplant — a living-donor kidney
          from a close friend. It was a second chance most people never get. But FSGS, the
          disease that had taken his own kidneys, recurred in the transplanted one. By 2014, he
          was back on dialysis, three times a week.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2019, Robert posted on Facebook asking if anyone knew someone willing to donate a
          kidney. Within about a week, organ matchmaker Chaya Lipschutz had found a potential
          match: Elizabeth Antosy. A transplant was scheduled for December 19, 2019.
        </p>
        <p>
          The night before surgery, transplant surgeon Dr. Stuart Greenstein called with the
          news: Robert had been found to have kidney cancer. The transplant could not go forward.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="pull">
        Elizabeth was heartbroken — but she refused to give up on giving Robert a kidney. Instead
        of walking away, she joined a kidney exchange chain.
      </p>

      <div>
        <p>
          Through that chain, Robert was matched with a new donor: Jen Keller. After he cleared
          cancer, Robert and Jen were transplanted on October 20, 2021. Elizabeth's decision to
          join the chain rather than step back didn't just help Robert — it set off a sequence of
          matches that ultimately gave <strong>8 people</strong> new kidneys.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="on-light section-tight" style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="h2">Where the name comes from.</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The Jentosy Project</strong> is named for the two women who made that second
        transplant possible: <strong>Jen</strong> Keller, Robert's donor, and Elizabeth
        An<strong>tosy</strong>, whose decision to join the exchange chain made the match happen
        at all. The organization exists to pay their gift forward.
      </p>
      <p class="linkline">
        Independently covered by
        <a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/newyork/news/kidney-transplant-kidney-chain-robert-sanchez-elizabeth-antosy/">CBS News New York</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="on-light section-tight" style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 class="h2">Why this became a mission, not just a memory.</h2>
      <p>
        Robert's path to founding The Jentosy Project didn't begin in a hospital. Before he
        became a social worker, he spent 15 years incarcerated on drug charges — an experience
        that, along with the poverty and instability he witnessed growing up, shaped his
        understanding of how deeply health and circumstance are linked. When FSGS took his
        kidneys years later, he recognized the same pattern he'd spent his career trying to
        interrupt: that the body often pays the price for systems that fail people first.
      </p>
      <p>
        That conviction — that kidney disease disparities can't be separated from poverty,
        education, incarceration, and the stress they produce —
        <a href="/the-need/">is the foundation of The Jentosy Project's mission</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="on-dark section-tight cta-band">
    <div class="container cta-inner reveal">
      <h2 class="h2">Help extend what Jen and Elizabeth started.</h2>
      <div class="cluster gap-3" style="margin-top:var(--space-5);">
        <a href="/partner-with-us/" class="btn btn-steel-outline">Partner With Us</a>
        <a href="/donate/" class="btn btn-rust">Donate</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .narrow {
    max-width: 700px;
  }

  .portrait-block {
    max-width: 240px;
  }

  .portrait-block .linkline {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .cta-inner {
    text-align: center;
    max-width: 640px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>
